CASE = [
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> Faxian, a Chinese Buddhist monk, travelled to India in the early 5th century CE to visit holy sites and collect Buddhist texts. He wrote an account of his journey, describing the kingdoms he saw and the monasteries where he stayed. "
   "(i) Which country did Faxian come from? (ii) Why did he travel to India? (iii) During whose reign did he visit? (iv) Why should a historian compare Faxian's account with other sources?",
   "(i) China (1). (ii) To visit Buddhist sites and collect texts (1). (iii) Chandragupta II (1). (iv) A traveller records only what he saw and may be biased or mistaken (1); comparing with inscriptions and coins confirms facts and gives a fuller picture (1).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> Aryabhata, who wrote the Aryabhatiya, explained that the Earth rotates on its axis, which causes day and night. Varahamihira, an astronomer at Ujjayini, compiled the Brihat Samhita on many subjects. Physicians such as Sushruta wrote on surgery. "
   "(i) Name the work of Aryabhata. (ii) What does the Earth's rotation cause, according to him? (iii) Who wrote about surgery? (iv) Why is the Gupta age called a classical age for science?",
   "(i) The Aryabhatiya (1). (ii) Day and night (1). (iii) Sushruta (1). (iv) Scholars made important advances in mathematics, astronomy and medicine (1); the peace and royal patronage of the period supported learning (1).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> Prabhavati Gupta, daughter of Chandragupta II, married into the Vakataka dynasty, which ruled from near Nagpur in the Deccan. She ruled as regent for her young sons after her husband's death. This alliance joined Gupta power with the Deccan. "
   "(i) Whose daughter was Prabhavati Gupta? (ii) Into which dynasty did she marry? (iii) What does regent mean? (iv) What does her story tell us about women in this period?",
   "(i) Chandragupta II (1). (ii) The Vakataka dynasty (1). (iii) One who rules on behalf of a child ruler (1). (iv) Royal women could hold real power and take decisions (1); marriage alliances were a way of building political ties (1).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

